# HAS A BUILD GOT SLOWER, IS A JOB NEAR ITS CEILING, IS MAIN WAITING FOR A RUNNER -- asked of the build timings every
# workflow pushes as its last job (scripts/ci/ci_timings.py), so nobody first hears of a slowdown when a job hits its
# `timeout-minutes` and goes red.
#
# WHY. 4b2e9cb04 made the convergence `takes` rows slower -- Poland 19-33 min to past its 55-minute ceiling, the UK
# 34-42 to past it, the US 27-35 to 46.5 -- and the first anyone heard was run 1641 timing out. Main's own end to end
# went from 28 to 42 minutes over a few days, and a recording's Germany leg from 18 to 125, with no word either. The
# "CI — build timings" dashboard drew all of it; nobody was looking.
#
# THE SERIES (ci_timings.py's header), one sample per run, every one stamped at the RUN'S START and pushed when the run
# ends -- hours later for a convergence leg. So nothing here reads a raw selector (its 5-minute lookback would never see
# a sample): every read is a range function over a window wider than the longest run.
#
#   kinowo_ci_job_seconds{workflow,ref,ci_job,conclusion,runner}  a job's wall clock
#   kinowo_ci_job_queue_seconds{...as job_seconds}            created -> started (dashboard only, no rule reads it)
#   kinowo_ci_step_seconds{workflow,ref,ci_job,step,runner}   a step's
#   kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow,ref,measure,conclusion}    `wall` (first job start to last job end), `runner_wait`
#   kinowo_ci_job_timeout_seconds{workflow,ref,ci_job,runner} the job's own `timeout-minutes`
#   kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow,ref}                        the run's index, the number in its URL
#
# `runner` is `ci-runner` (self-hosted), `hosted` or `unknown`. A job that moves runner starts a new series, with its
# own baseline (a self-hosted runner is not judged against GitHub's); every pairing below (`> baseline`, `/ timeout`)
# matches on all labels, so it pairs a job with its own runner kind. Samples before 2026-11-06 have no `runner`.
#
# `ref` is "main" or "branch". No commit SHA is a label (one series per run); the run number is a sample VALUE, and the
# regression alerts read it to name the runs a regression started between: the last run at baseline and the first slow
# one after it. Their commits are the range.
#
# THE BASELINE is each series' own: the median of its SUCCESSFUL main runs from 16 to 2 days ago, given at least three.
# Successful only: a failed or cancelled job stopped early, and its short time would drag the median down. Ending two
# days back keeps the runs being judged out of the baseline that judges them, and a regression that lasts becomes the
# new baseline once it is half of the fortnight -- by then it has been reported for a week.
#
# A REGRESSION is a run over baseline + max(40%, 5 minutes): the ratio because legs vary +-25% run to run (Poland's
# takes 19-33 around 26), the floor so a 40-second job taking a minute is not news. It fires when the LATEST successful
# run and at least one other in the last two days are over it -- two runs, so one slow runner is not a regression, and
# the latest, so a fix clears it with its first run -- or at once for one run over twice its baseline and 10 minutes
# more (Germany's 18 -> 125). Against the motivating runs: US takes 46.5 over a 31 median fires (threshold 43.4), Main
# 42 over 28 fires (39.2), Poland's 33 over 26 does not (36.4).
#
# COUNTING RUNS INSIDE A WINDOW. `count_over_time((last_over_time(x[1m]) > t)[2d:1m])` counts each sample once: a
# subquery step of one minute over a one-minute left-open range sees every sample in exactly one step (a raw `x` would
# repeat each for five, its lookback). The threshold is read `@ end()` -- today's, for every step -- so it is looked
# up once rather than once a minute.
groups:
  - name: ci-build-times
    # Every rule reads days of a sparse series; nothing here needs to be fresher than a run is long.
    interval: 5m
    rules:
      - record: ci:job_seconds:baseline
        expr: >-
          quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[14d] offset 2d)
          and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[14d] offset 2d) >= 3
      - record: ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold
        expr: ci:job_seconds:baseline + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:job_seconds:baseline, 300)

      - record: ci:step_seconds:baseline
        expr: >-
          quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[14d] offset 2d)
          and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[14d] offset 2d) >= 3
      - record: ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold
        expr: ci:step_seconds:baseline + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:step_seconds:baseline, 300)

      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline
        expr: >-
          quantile_over_time(0.5, kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[14d] offset 2d)
          and count_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[14d] offset 2d) >= 3
      - record: ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold
        expr: ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline + clamp_min(0.4 * ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline, 300)

      # A WHOLE RUN, first job start to last job end: Main's 28 -> 42 minutes.
      - alert: CiRunWallClockRegressed
        expr: >-
          (
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[2d])
              > ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold
            and
            count_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[1m])
              > ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold @ end())[2d:1m]) >= 2
          )
          or
          (
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[2d])
              > 2 * ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline
            and
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[2d])
              - ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline > 600
          )
        # HELD SIX HOURS PAST THE LAST RUN THAT SHOWED IT. Each main run re-judges the latest sample, so
        # a regression resolved and re-fired run by run (median episode 1.8h, 28 flaps a week).
        keep_firing_for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} on main got slower: {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} end to end"
          description: >-
            The latest successful main run of {{ $labels.workflow }} took {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} from its first
            job's start to its last job's end, against a median of
            {{ with printf `ci:run_wall_seconds:baseline{workflow=%q}` $labels.workflow | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanizeDuration }}{{ end }}
            over the fortnight before the last two days.
            {{ with printf `max_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[5m]) and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[5m]) <= ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()))[16d:5m])` $labels.workflow $labels.workflow | query }}{{ $good := . | first | value }}Last run at baseline #{{ printf "%.0f" $good }}{{ with printf `min_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[5m]) > %f and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",measure="wall",conclusion="success"}[5m]) > ci:run_wall_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()))[16d:5m])` $labels.workflow $good $labels.workflow | query }}, first slow run after it #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ end }}: the regression is in the commits between them.{{ else }}No run in 16 days was at baseline.{{ end }}
            Jobs regressed in it (their own alerts are inhibited by this one):
            {{ range printf `ALERTS{alertname="CiJobDurationRegressed",alertstate="firing",workflow=%q}` $labels.workflow | query }}{{ .Labels.ci_job }}; {{ else }}none.{{ end }}
            Compare the two runs' jobs on the "CI — build timings" dashboard.

      # A JOB: the convergence `takes` rows, a capture leg, a CI shard.
      - alert: CiJobDurationRegressed
        expr: >-
          (
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[2d])
              > ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold
            and
            count_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[1m])
              > ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold @ end())[2d:1m]) >= 2
          )
          or
          (
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[2d]) > 2 * ci:job_seconds:baseline
            and
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main",conclusion="success"}[2d]) - ci:job_seconds:baseline > 600
          )
        # HELD SIX HOURS PAST THE LAST RUN THAT SHOWED IT. Each main run re-judges the latest sample, so
        # a regression resolved and re-fired run by run (median episode 1.8h, 28 flaps a week).
        keep_firing_for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} / {{ $labels.ci_job }} on main got slower: {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            The latest successful main run of {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.workflow }}) took
            {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, against a median of
            {{ with printf `ci:job_seconds:baseline{workflow=%q,ci_job=%q}` $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanizeDuration }}{{ end }}
            over the fortnight before the last two days.
            {{ with printf `max_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[5m]) and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",conclusion="success",ci_job=%q}[5m]) <= ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()))[16d:5m])` $labels.workflow $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ $good := . | first | value }}Last run at baseline #{{ printf "%.0f" $good }}{{ with printf `min_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[5m]) > %f and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",conclusion="success",ci_job=%q}[5m]) > ci:job_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()))[16d:5m])` $labels.workflow $good $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job | query }}, first slow run after it #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ end }}: the regression is in the commits between them.{{ else }}No run in 16 days was at baseline.{{ end }}
            Steps regressed in it (their own alerts are inhibited by this one):
            {{ range printf `ALERTS{alertname="CiStepDurationRegressed",alertstate="firing",workflow=%q,ci_job=%q}` $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ .Labels.step }}; {{ else }}none.{{ end }}
            Every job of a workflow slowing at once is the runner host, not a commit. A job that times out has no
            successful run to judge: CiJobNearTimeout is the alert for that.

      # A STEP, which says what inside the job grew. Every step is a candidate; the 5-minute floor keeps the setup and
      # upload steps out unless one really grows.
      - alert: CiStepDurationRegressed
        expr: >-
          (
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[2d]) > ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold
            and
            count_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[1m])
              > ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold @ end())[2d:1m]) >= 2
          )
          or
          (
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[2d]) > 2 * ci:step_seconds:baseline
            and
            last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{ref="main"}[2d]) - ci:step_seconds:baseline > 600
          )
        # HELD SIX HOURS PAST THE LAST RUN THAT SHOWED IT. Each main run re-judges the latest sample, so
        # a regression resolved and re-fired run by run (median episode 1.8h, 28 flaps a week).
        keep_firing_for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} / {{ $labels.ci_job }} step `{{ $labels.step }}` on main got slower: {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            The latest main run of the step `{{ $labels.step }}` in {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.workflow }}) took
            {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, against a median of
            {{ with printf `ci:step_seconds:baseline{workflow=%q,ci_job=%q,step=%q}` $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job $labels.step | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanizeDuration }}{{ end }}
            over the fortnight before the last two days.
            {{ with printf `max_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[5m]) and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",ci_job=%q,step=%q}[5m]) <= ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()))[16d:5m])` $labels.workflow $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job $labels.step | query }}{{ $good := . | first | value }}Last run at baseline #{{ printf "%.0f" $good }}{{ with printf `min_over_time((last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref="main"}[5m]) > %f and on () (last_over_time(kinowo_ci_step_seconds{workflow=%q,ref="main",ci_job=%q,step=%q}[5m]) > ci:step_seconds:regression_threshold @ end()))[16d:5m])` $labels.workflow $good $labels.workflow $labels.ci_job $labels.step | query }}, first slow run after it #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ end }}: the regression is in the commits between them.{{ else }}No run in 16 days was at baseline.{{ end }}

      # WITHIN 80% OF ITS CEILING, whatever the job's conclusion: a job that hit its `timeout-minutes` is cancelled or
      # failed, and that is the run this has to see -- before it, ideally, as US takes at 46.5 of 55 would have been.
      # Main's jobs, and the identity landing checks' (Identity model convergence on measure/ branches), whose takes
      # rows are the ones that timed out; other branch runs are experiments, slow on purpose as often as not.
      # The latest run only: a job's samples of every conclusion, newest first, so a later run back under it clears it.
      - alert: CiJobNearTimeout
        expr: >-
          last_over_time(max without (conclusion) (
              last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="main"}[1m])
              or last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_seconds{ref="branch",workflow="Identity model convergence"}[1m])
            )[1d:1m])
          >= 0.8 * last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_timeout_seconds[1d])
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.workflow }} / {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.ref }}) took {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}, within 80% of its timeout"
          description: >-
            {{ $labels.ci_job }} ({{ $labels.workflow }}, {{ $labels.ref }}) took {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} in
            {{ with printf `last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow=%q,ref=%q}[1d])` $labels.workflow $labels.ref | query }}run #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ else }}its latest run{{ end }},
            against a `timeout-minutes` of
            {{ with printf `last_over_time(kinowo_ci_job_timeout_seconds{workflow=%q,ref=%q,ci_job=%q}[1d])` $labels.workflow $labels.ref $labels.ci_job | query }}{{ . | first | value | humanizeDuration }}{{ end }}.
            The next slowdown times it out and turns the run red. Find what grew (CiJobDurationRegressed, or the job's
            steps and phases on the "CI — build timings" dashboard) before raising the ceiling: a leg near its limit is
            a signal to re-measure, not to raise the number again (country-convergence-leg.yml).

      # MAIN'S FIRST JOB WAITING FOR A RUNNER, not for the run ahead of it (Main runs one at a time; ci_timings.py
      # `ready_at`). The `main-lane` tier is Main's alone and the target is under 2 minutes: over 5 on two runs in six
      # hours, or 15 once, is the tier short of runners or a runner down.
      - alert: MainCiRunnerWaitHigh
        expr: >-
          (
            last_over_time(max without (conclusion) (
                last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow="Main",ref="main",measure="runner_wait"}[1m])
              )[6h:1m]) > 300
            and
            count_over_time((max without (conclusion) (
                last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow="Main",ref="main",measure="runner_wait"}[1m])
              ) > 300)[6h:1m]) >= 2
          )
          or
          last_over_time(max without (conclusion) (
              last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_seconds{workflow="Main",ref="main",measure="runner_wait"}[1m])
            )[6h:1m]) > 900
        labels:
          severity: warning
          domain: ci
        annotations:
          summary: "Main's first job waited {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} for a runner"
          description: >-
            {{ with query `last_over_time(kinowo_ci_run_number{workflow="Main",ref="main"}[6h])` }}Run #{{ . | first | value | printf "%.0f" }}{{ else }}Main's latest run{{ end }}
            waited {{ $value | humanizeDuration }} from when it could start to its first job starting, against a
            target of under 2 minutes. Check the `main-lane` runners on ci-runner (infra forgejo.nix `ciTiers`): one
            down, or a job from another workflow holding the label.
